## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Interview Context
This question tests distributed processing, memory management, and algorithmic optimization skills for senior engineering roles dealing with big data scenarios.

## Technical Approach
- **Memory Mapping**: Use `mmap` for zero-copy file access, avoiding data duplication
- **Streaming Algorithm**: Process file in fixed-size chunks (64KB-1MB) with bounded memory
- **Data Structure**: Min-heap (O(log n)) for top-10 maintenance vs full sort (O(n log n))
- **Parallel Processing**: Split file into N chunks, process concurrently, then merge results

## Go Implementation
```go
type IPCounter struct {
    counts map[string]int
    heap   *MinHeap
    mu     sync.RWMutex
}

func (ic *IPCounter) processChunk(chunk []byte) {
    scanner := bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(chunk))
    for scanner.Scan() {
        ip := extractIP(scanner.Text())
        ic.mu.Lock()
        ic.counts[ip]++
        ic.mu.Unlock()
    }
}

func (ic *IPCounter) getTop10() []IPCount {
    heap := container.NewMinHeap(10)
    for ip, count := range ic.counts {
        if heap.Len() < 10 {
            heap.Push(IPCount{ip, count})
        } else if count > heap.Peek().Count {
            heap.Pop()
            heap.Push(IPCount{ip, count})
        }
    }
    return heap.ToSlice()
}
```

